1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,005
WHO CLEANS UP AFTER THE REVOLUTION?
The hidden labor of an experimental theater

2
00:00:05,305 --> 00:00:10,505
A GROUP BEGINS IN A ROOM

3
00:00:13,005 --> 00:00:15,837
A performance begins when the lights come on.

4
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:19,377
A community begins much earlier, and may end much later.

5
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,563
Before there is a stage, someone opens the door.

6
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:23,626
Someone arrives first.

7
00:00:23,626 --> 00:00:30,706
Someone waits for the others, finds the missing chair, changes the
plan, and decides that tonight is still worth attempting.

8
00:00:30,706 --> 00:00:36,370
In the rehearsal room for Iceberg, the visible action is one body
searching for a role.

9
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:42,034
But another drama is already present: who makes the conditions in
which that search can continue?

10
00:00:42,034 --> 00:00:44,513
We usually call the first part art.

11
00:00:44,513 --> 00:00:46,991
The second part disappears into the room.

12
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:10,532
THE INVISIBLE REHEARSAL

13
00:01:13,032 --> 00:01:14,899
Rehearsal often looks like conversation.

14
00:01:14,899 --> 00:01:17,513
Underneath it is a distribution of permission.

15
00:01:17,513 --> 00:01:18,633
Who may interrupt?

16
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:20,874
Whose uncertainty becomes a new direction?

17
00:01:20,874 --> 00:01:23,488
Whose exhaustion is treated as an obstacle?

18
00:01:23,488 --> 00:01:33,198
A group can sit in a circle and still depend on one person for the
key, the schedule, the camera, the money, or the final decision.

19
00:01:33,198 --> 00:01:36,185
Equality is not a shape made by chairs.

20
00:01:36,185 --> 00:01:45,148
It is a repeated practice: information moves, decisions can be
questioned, and responsibility does not fall forever on the quietest
or most available person.

21
00:02:05,358 --> 00:02:10,558
INTENSITY IS NOT EQUALITY

22
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:14,900
Then the lights change everything.

23
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:16,742
On stage, proximity becomes intensity.

24
00:02:16,742 --> 00:02:20,793
Separate bodies appear to share one risk, one rhythm, one world.

25
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:25,950
This is one of theater's great powers, and one of its most
convincing illusions.

26
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:28,528
Intensity can be real without being equal.

27
00:02:28,528 --> 00:02:36,631
A powerful scene does not tell us who cleaned the room, who absorbed
the conflict, or who could refuse the next rehearsal.

28
00:02:36,631 --> 00:02:38,473
The audience sees the event.

29
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:42,524
The community has to live with the conditions that produced it.

30
00:03:05,385 --> 00:03:10,585
WHO GETS TO STOP?

31
00:03:13,085 --> 00:03:15,525
Every experiment praises the courage to begin.

32
00:03:15,525 --> 00:03:17,966
Fewer experiments design the right to stop.

33
00:03:17,966 --> 00:03:25,287
What happens when a performer no longer wants the role, the image,
or the story that the group tells about them?

34
00:03:25,287 --> 00:03:29,470
Can they leave with their name, their material, and their dignity
intact?

35
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:32,259
Exit is not the failure of a community.

36
00:03:32,259 --> 00:03:36,443
Sometimes it is the clearest test of whether participation was ever
voluntary.

37
00:03:36,443 --> 00:03:41,672
A group that welcomes everyone but punishes departure has built an
entrance, not a freedom.

38
00:04:05,412 --> 00:04:10,612
AFTER THE APPLAUSE

39
00:04:13,112 --> 00:04:14,904
Applause can close a performance.

40
00:04:14,904 --> 00:04:16,696
It cannot close the account.

41
00:04:16,696 --> 00:04:27,451
After the audience leaves, there are files to return, equipment to
carry, messages to answer, money to explain, floors to clean, and
relationships that may not fit the final poster.

42
00:04:27,451 --> 00:04:29,961
This labor is not outside the artwork.

43
00:04:29,961 --> 00:04:34,621
It determines whether another artwork can begin without consuming
the same people again.

44
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:44,659
The most radical gesture may be neither a speech nor an image, but a
schedule that shares the burden and an archive that allows
disagreement to remain visible.

45
00:05:05,438 --> 00:05:10,638
THE REAL TEST OF A COMMUNITY

46
00:05:13,138 --> 00:05:16,176
So what does a community owe its members?

47
00:05:16,176 --> 00:05:17,315
Not permanent harmony.

48
00:05:17,315 --> 00:05:20,352
Not the promise that nobody will be hurt.

49
00:05:20,352 --> 00:05:27,947
It owes them conditions that can be examined: who decides, who
maintains, who is heard, and how someone can leave.

50
00:05:27,947 --> 00:05:35,920
Iceberg survives in the archive not because it gives one answer, but
because rehearsal, performance, criticism, and aftermath remain in
tension.

51
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:40,477
The real test of a community is not how intensely people gather.

52
00:05:40,477 --> 00:05:46,552
It is how maintenance, conflict, and exit are actually carried
together, after the light moves on.

53
00:06:05,165 --> 00:06:11,171
P4 THEATER — ICEBERG
Rehearsal / Performance / Archive

